#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060776} :activity/id #uuid "b70e2d1f-56a3-5e7e-b1dd-0d8ac4b754e0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043 :topic/id #uuid "7cda6484-cda1-5805-8a40-963bb1a60fdf" :topic/math-academy-id 204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060861 :tutorial/content "We can use reference angles to evaluate trigonometric ratios for angles outside the first quadrant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the value of $\\cos (135^\\circ)$, corresponding to the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the unit circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/bef50a070785e4bb86867ba6404942fd7eaa04b60720adfb04176bafe2fa2121.png)\n\nThe reference angle for\n\n$$\n\\theta=135^\\circ\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=45^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33b208e44aaa556a67ebaf7a6c1117b09adf863c56deb1bc1f5590e289062c4b.png)\n\nLet $P'$ be the image of $P$ under a reflection across the $y$-axis. This gives a pair of congruent angles of measure $45^\\circ$, as shown.\n\n![](images/c4/c4875682498edaadc74a8af238fdf3293b4820025256df78e60867940061785e.png)\n\nAccording to the definition, $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ is the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$. But this is the same as the $x$-coordinate of the point $P'$ *taken with the opposite sign.*\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of $P'$ equals the cosine of the reference angle.\n\n$$\n\\cos(45^\\circ)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\, \\underbrace{135^\\circ}_{\\theta} \\,) =-\\cos(\\, \\underbrace{45^\\circ}_{\\theta_R} \\,) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we simplified the evaluation of $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ by evaluating the cosine of the corresponding reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R=45^\\circ\n$$\n\n*with an additional sign adjustment* for the final result.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to show that, since\n\n$$\ny = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sin(135^\\circ) = \\sin(45^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we do **NOT** change the sign because the $y$-coordinates of $P'$ and $P$ *are equal.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c1461a2-d5b4-5d11-b0ad-6537ca726992" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10532 :tutorial/title "Calculating Trigonometric Ratios Using Reference Angles"} :step/id #uuid "bdeb7825-3a44-52de-9875-55a1ffbcd7e7" :step/math-academy-id 210844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060971 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the cosine and sine ratios for *acute* angles can be defined in the following ways:\n\n- Using a right triangle. If $\\theta$ is an acute angle in a right triangle, then\n$\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}$.\n- Using the unit circle. If $\\theta$ is a central angle made by a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle in the first quadrant, then\n$\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y$.\n\nWe can't define trigonometric ratios for non-acute angles using right triangles because a right triangle can't have angles larger than $90^\\circ$. However, we can *extend* these ratios for non-acute angles using unit circles.\n\nTo do that, let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in any quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/61/612d468ea23fefb6bbb33bd676a1930604b283475f36be08541419776bb580bd.png)\n\nWe *define* the sine and cosine of $\\theta$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the cosine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $x$-coordinate of $P$, and\n- the sine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $y$-coordinate of $P$.\n\nThis way, our trigonometric ratios make sense for any angle, even if this angle is larger than $90^\\circ$." :tutorial/id #uuid "67345ec4-5f02-5d0a-9900-417059fb1ba3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a1c1a6d-1eee-5229-b88d-9d28c1ae4c0a" :step/math-academy-id 40995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059389 :question/id #uuid "ec8644e2-c365-5dce-bb21-7a148203a03a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10818"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1810cacd-3565-5438-ae93-45b50970f5dd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060328}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "c87cdc7f-1883-5d6b-8cb5-f78979ccbb35" :step/math-academy-id 40996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060972 :tutorial/content "We can calculate a trigonometric ratio of *any* angle $\\theta$ by finding the ratio of the corresponding reference angle and adjusting the sign depending on which quadrant our angle lies in.\n\nTo do this, we perform the following steps:\n\n1. Find the reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the ratio for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting ratio is positive or negative.\n\nWe must do the last step because the ratio for $\\theta_R$ will always be nonnegative, but the ratio for $\\theta$ may not be.\n\nBut how do we determine whether the ratio for $\\theta$ is positive or negative? This depends on the trigonometric ratio and which quadrant the angle is in.\n\nTo help us remember when various trigonometric ratios are positive, we use the mnemonic \"**A**ll **S**tudents **T**ake **C**alculus.\"\n\n- **A**ll of the trigonometric ratios are positive in quadrant I\n- **S**ine (and only sine) is positive in quadrant II\n- **T**angent (and only tangent) is positive in quadrant III\n- **C**osine (and only cosine) is positive in quadrant IV\n\nWe can represent this in a so-called CAST diagram, shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a6d43985058f1afd33272537def49886177aceab115f0084b1cf9d08ba78c43.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider $\\sin 140^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/da/da7ca1a89b91d157ad97a3800d8c2d67efb467a8ceaa8f3c964bea5275ecfc5d.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = 140^\\circ\n$$\n\nis the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 180^{∘} - θ \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 140^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin{140^\\circ}$, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin{40^\\circ}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin{140^\\circ}$ must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin{40^\\circ}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "807bb7a1-70c9-500c-be69-53b15b903672" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6763 :tutorial/title "The CAST Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "55b9e1e7-d450-5f66-8313-91113548a2fe" :step/math-academy-id 41000 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059390 :question/id #uuid "ea7f4d67-8d88-559f-b39e-7618d86f9379" :question/math-academy-id "e-10819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b43c0b-f0bb-54eb-9644-6db9398ab0a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050795}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060075}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "b9889a1e-5c54-5abd-b023-829bf9ee0adc" :step/math-academy-id 41002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059391 :question/id #uuid "926813b0-e2fb-5671-8b99-a00c4710c4d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "669295c7-5c63-5926-907c-1e2c211c3570" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060330}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "99d08952-d027-5e02-b60d-55b149c7a7ca" :step/math-academy-id 41006 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060781}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059392 :question/id #uuid "0561b4af-2393-5bc1-99e0-9c996f13c4a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10822"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81fb8ad6-b9b7-5d2b-9ea5-17c6df4dea68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060427}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "6ead1064-5647-5f6e-a0fb-17f9f615df2b" :step/math-academy-id 41009}] :activity/title "Extending the Trigonometric Ratios Using the Unit Circle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059904}]}
